{"question":"Do the day 11–25 pendulum_lab readings reflect a genuine upward calibration shift from the day 1–10 baseline, or are the elevated values explained by ordinary scatter plus a transient local disturbance/drift affecting some runs?",
"hypotheses":[{"label":"The pendulum instrument is still fundamentally stable; the post-baseline readings are ordinary scatter around the day 1–10 baseline mean.","probability":0.52,"rationale":"This remains the default because the post-baseline series is not cleanly separated from baseline behavior: many individual trials still land near the baseline neighborhood, and the recent values on days 24–25 continue to oscillate rather than form a monotone rise. A sizeable z-score shows the mean difference is notable, but a mean offset alone does not compel a regime change when the within-series pattern is mixed and the instrument has a substantial trial count. The new day 24–25 observations are especially important because they include both low-ish and high-ish values around the same range, which looks like scatter rather than a consistent step shift.","evidenceFor":[298,299,300,301,302,303,304,305,313,314,315,316,317,318,319,320],"evidenceAgainst":[275,283,284,285,286,287,288,289,290]},
{"label":"There is a small real upward shift in pendulum_lab, likely from instrument drift or a local environmental influence rather than a new physical regime.","probability":0.35,"rationale":"The post-baseline mean remains higher than the day 1–10 baseline, and the elevation is not confined to a single isolated spike: the day 22–25 readings include multiple values above the baseline center. The size of the effect is modest, but the persistence across several days argues against pure one-off noise. The latest data do not eliminate this possibility; instead they show continued recurrence of slightly elevated readings alongside near-baseline ones, which is consistent with a small drift superposed on normal scatter.","evidenceFor":[275,283,287,288,289,290,301,302,304,305,315,317,318,319,320],"evidenceAgainst":[298,299,300,303,313,314,316]},
{"label":"A temporary procedural or environmental disturbance is intermittently inflating pendulum readings without a persistent calibration shift.","probability":0.08,"rationale":"Intermittent disturbance can naturally produce patchy elevations mixed with returns toward baseline, which matches the alternating high/near-baseline pattern seen in the recent trials. However, because elevated values recur across multiple days rather than being tightly localized to one short burst, a purely transient disturbance is less compelling than a small ongoing drift or simple scatter. I keep this below the stable-shift hypothesis because the evidence suggests repetition, not just a single short-lived episode.","evidenceFor":[286,287,288,289,290,301,302,304,305,315,317,318,319,320],"evidenceAgainst":[298,299,300,303,313,314,316]},
{"label":"The baseline mean from days 1–10 is somewhat unrepresentative, so the apparent shift is overstated.","probability":0.03,"rationale":"A slightly atypical baseline could exaggerate the contrast with later readings, especially if early conditions were unusually favorable. But the baseline campaign is already fairly well populated, and the later data do not directly show a clear reason to distrust the early mean. This remains a bookkeeping possibility rather than a leading physical explanation.","evidenceFor":[275,283,284,285,286,287,288,289,290],"evidenceAgainst":[298,299,300,301,302,303,304,305,313,314,315,316,317,318,319,320]},
{"label":"Some other unconsidered cause explains the pendulum pattern.","probability":0.02,"rationale":"This covers hidden calibration changes, unnoticed maintenance effects, or an unlogged environmental correlate not yet captured in the current framing. I do not currently see evidence strong enough to force a new category, but it remains prudent to reserve a small probability for an unknown cause.","evidenceFor":[275,283,284,285,286,287,288,289,290,298,299,300,301,302,303,304,305,313,314,315,316,317,318,319,320],"evidenceAgainst":[]}
],"residual":0.0,"summaryOfChange":"I kept the question focused on the pendulum anomaly itself, because the new day 24–25 readings do not create a new problem larger than the existing one. Compared with the prior review, I moved probability away from the 'stable scatter' view and modestly toward a genuine small upward shift, because the elevated readings continue through day 25 and recur across several days. I also slightly reduced the odds of a purely transient disturbance, since the pattern now looks more recurrent than episodic. The baseline-misrepresentation and unknown-cause categories remain low-probability bookkeeping alternatives."}
